	// We want to pick quantities x_t such that sum x_t <= k, 0 <= x_t <= c[i][t], maximizing sum (b[j][t] - a[i][t]) * x_t.
	// This is a fractional knapsack-like problem but with integer constraints. However, since all profits are linear and we can take any integer quantity up to c[i][t], the optimal is to take as many as possible of the items with highest profit per unit, then fill the remaining capacity with the next best, etc.
	// Actually, since we can take any integer number up to c[i][t], and the profit per unit is constant for each item type, the greedy approach works: sort item types by profit per unit descending, take min(c[i][t], remaining_capacity) of each.

